{"step_index":18,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-18T19:49:36Z","content":"## The Stack — What Each Tool Is & What It's Used For\n\n---\n\n### **Next.js**\n**What is it?** A JavaScript/React framework for building websites and web apps. Think of it as the \"skeleton\" that makes the entire platform — the pages you see, the buttons you click, the backend logic that processes data.\n\n**What is it used for?** Building the full platform UI — the dashboard where his team manages videos, thumbnails, emails, boards, reporting. Both the frontend (what users see) and the backend API (what handles data behind the scenes) live here.\n\n---\n\n### **Postgres (PostgreSQL)**\n**What is it?** A database. Like a giant organized spreadsheet that stores all the data — but way more powerful. It's one of the most trusted databases in the industry.\n\n**What is it used for?** Storing everything: video projects, thumbnail data, user accounts, email campaigns, job queue status, AI agent results, analytics. When he says \"180 migrations,\" that means the database structure has been modified 180 times as the platform evolved — that's a LOT of data structure.\n\n---\n\n### **Docker**\n**What is it?** A tool that packages your entire app (code + dependencies + FFmpeg + whisper.cpp + everything) into a \"container\" — like a sealed box that runs the same way on any computer. No more \"it works on my machine\" problems.\n\n**What is it used for?** Running the whole platform reliably on a server. FFmpeg, whisper.cpp, Node.js, Postgres — all packaged together so deployment is clean and repeatable. If the server dies, you spin up the same Docker container elsewhere and everything works identically.\n\n---\n\n### **VPS (Virtual Private Server)**\n**What is it?** A rented server in the cloud. Unlike shared hosting (where you share resources with random people), a VPS gives you a dedicated chunk of a physical server with full control — your own operating system, your own storage, your own RAM.\n\n**What is it used for?** Hosting the entire platform. The Next.js app, Postgres database, Docker containers, FFmpeg rendering — it all runs on this VPS. It's cheaper than AWS/GCP for a single-developer operation.\n\n---\n\n### **GitHub Actions**\n**What is it?** An automation system built into GitHub. You write a set of instructions (called a \"workflow\"), and GitHub runs them automatically when triggered — like when code is pushed, or on a schedule, or manually.\n\n**What is it used for?** Two things likely: (1) **CI/CD** — automatically testing the code and deploying updates to the VPS when new code is pushed. (2) **Video rendering** — potentially offloading heavy FFmpeg render jobs to GitHub's free servers (2,000 minutes/month free compute). You already use this concept in your own Stickman pipeline.\n\n---\n\n### **Cloudflare**\n**What is it?** A company that sits between your users and your server. It acts as a shield and speed booster. When someone visits the platform, the request hits Cloudflare first, then gets forwarded to the VPS.\n\n**What is it used for?** Three things: (1) **CDN** — caching static files (images, CSS, JS) on servers worldwide so the platform loads fast everywhere. (2) **Security** — blocking DDoS attacks, bots, and malicious traffic before they reach the VPS. (3) **DNS** — managing the domain name so `platform.com` points to the right server.\n\n---\n\n### Quick Summary Table\n\n| Tool | Kya hai (one line) | Kaam kya karta hai |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Next.js** | Web app framework | Platform ka poora UI + backend |\n| **Postgres** | Database | Saara data store karta hai |\n| **Docker** | App packaging tool | Sab kuch ek box mein pack karke server pe chalaata hai |\n| **VPS** | Rented server | Platform host karta hai |\n| **GitHub Actions** | Automation runner | Code test, deploy, aur rendering automate karta hai |\n| **Cloudflare** | Security + speed layer | Platform ko fast aur safe banaata hai |","thinking":"The user wants two things explained for each tool in the stack: what it is, and what it's used for."}
